  "body": {
    "id": "thm:strict-equal-block-delta",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "When \\(n_B=n_S\\), \\(\\Phi_{n_B,n_S}(R)\\ge\\Phi_{n_B}^{\\mathrm{eq}}(R)\\). The containment is strict: at \\(n_B=n_S=16\\) and \\(R=4\\), \\(\\Phi_{16,16}(4)=452>256=\\Phi_{16}^{\\mathrm{eq}}(4)\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "Phi",
      "PhiEq",
      "R",
      "nB",
      "nS"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:affine-frontier",
      "prop:r4-reduction",
      "def:common-support",
      "def:support-size",
      "def:retention",
      "def:profile-class",
      "def:frontier",
      "def:equal-square-comparator"
    ],
    "justification": "A concrete strict delta distinguishes the catalog from a notational enlargement of the published equal-block family.",
    "gap": "The published equal-square calculation does not optimize over unequal block sizes even on a square population.",
    "consumer": "Researchers using the published \\(k\\)-block rule obtain a certified focal-size improvement without changing the randomization design.",
    "proof_tex": "Set \\(n_B=n_S=n\\).  For every positive divisor \\(k\\) of \\(n\\), take \\(L=2n/k\\) paired blocks with \\(a_\\ell=b_\\ell=k\\).  The covering equalities hold, its common half-sum assignments are exactly the subsets of \\([L]\\) of size \\(n/k\\), and hence\n\\[\n h(a,b)=\\binom{2n/k}{n/k},\n \\qquad\n F(a,b)=Lk^2=2nk.\n\\]\nWhenever the binomial coefficient is at least \\(R\\), this equal-square profile lies in \\(\\mathcal A_{n,n}(R)\\).  Maximizing over all eligible divisors proves\n\\[\n \\Phi_{n,n}(R)\\ge\\Phi_n^{\\mathrm{eq}}(R).\n\\]\nIf there is no eligible divisor, the right side is \\(-\\infty\\) by \\(\\mathrm{def{:}equal\\text{-}square\\text{-}comparator}\\), and the inequality remains valid.\n\nAt \\(n=16\\) and \\(R=4\\), \\(\\mathrm{prop{:}r4\\text{-}reduction}\\) gives\n\\[\n \\Phi_{16,16}(4)=2(15)^2+2=452.\n\\]\nThe positive divisors of \\(16\\) are \\(1,2,4,8,16\\).  The first four are eligible because their support sizes are respectively\n\\(\\binom{32}{16}\\), \\(\\binom{16}{8}\\), \\(\\binom84\\), and \\(\\binom42=6\\), whereas \\(k=16\\) has support \\(\\binom21=2<4\\).  The eligible retention values are \\(32,64,128,256\\), so\n\\[\n \\Phi_{16}^{\\mathrm{eq}}(4)=256<452=\\Phi_{16,16}(4).\n\\]\nThis proves both the general containment and the claimed strict instance.",
